  "id": "Compound::DB00692 - Gene::147",
  "verbalization": "Phentolamine, a selective alpha-1 adrenergic receptor antagonist, is shown to bind to Adra1d, a variant of the Adra1 receptor, which in turn participates in adrenergic receptor activity. This pathway links Phentolamine to Adra1b through a series of molecular interactions, suggesting a potential mechanism by which the drug may modulate adrenergic signaling. The evidence is consistent across multiple paths, with Adra1d serving as a central mediator in the interaction between Phentolamine and Adra1b. The observed scores indicate a strong, albeit variable, level of confidence in this relationship, supported by the shared participation of Adra1d in adrenergic receptor activity. The mechanism appears to involve Phentolamine's ability to inhibit alpha-1 adrenergic receptors, which are involved in the regulation of various physiological processes, including vascular tone and smooth muscle contraction. The integration of these pathways suggests that Phentolamine may exert its effects through a well-defined, mechanistic route involving Adra1d and Adra1b, though further experimental validation is needed to confirm the direct interaction between Phentolamine and Adra1b.",
